\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late Amid Renewed Pressure On Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2001\par 11:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Wednesday, as renewed pressure on the dollar helped them sustain their recent gains despite the region's grim economic outlook, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve's decision Tuesday to trim short-term interest rates by a quarter percentage point was widely expected, and had little impact on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Instead, the market focused on the Fed's statement accompanying its action, which suggested that the U.S. economy hasn't responded to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attempts to spur growth. The Fed's latest move marked the seventh cut this year, bringing interest rates to their lowest level in seven years. \par \par \uc2  The Fed also indicated it was more likely to lower than raise rates in the future, saying risks were "weighted mainly toward . . . economic weakness" rather than inflation. \par \par \uc2  "The repeat of the June statement emphasized the fact that business capital spending continues to weaken, suggesting that no upturn is in sight for the high-tech industry that fuels the region's exports," Bank of America said in a report. \par \par \uc2  Repeated verbal warnings by Japanese officials against excessive yen strength did little to offset the pressure on the dollar, which slid overnight following the Fed's announcement. \par \par \uc2  At 0820 GMT (4:20 a.m. EST), the dollar was quoted at Y119.69, slightly higher than Y119.55 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y120.68 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Despite the yen's resilience, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  won from breaching past KRW1,280 to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In the case of the Singapore dollar, talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported above S$1.7500, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, the dollar finished at 1,282.70 won, down from KRW1,283.80 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After the market closed, the South Korean government said it has reached an agreement to sell large stakes in Hyundai Investment Trust & Securities Co. - and its units, Hyundai Investment Trust Management Co. and Hyundai Securities Co. (Q.HDS) - to a consortium led by American International Group Inc. (AIG). \par \par \uc2  The government's Financial Supervisory Commission will hold a press conference Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The deal is expected to bring $800 million to $1 billion worth of inflows into South Korea, supporting the won, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's weakness against the yen and the euro failed to inspire the Singapore dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation by some dealers that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had checked the local dollar's advance when it climbed to as high as S$1.7475 in New York trade Tuesday kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  afloat above S$1.7500 Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar briefly received an additional boost late in the day, jumping to around S$1.7540, on the Australian government's approval of Singapore Telecommunication Ltd's (P.SGT) A$14 billion-plus takeover bid for Australia's second-largest telecommunications group, Cable & Wireless Optus Ltd. (A.CWO). \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to sustain its gains, and subsequently retreated to S$1.7525, weaker than late Tuesday's level. \par \par \uc2  "There were keen sellers around the S$1.7450 to S$1.7550 level," said a senior dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was the main beneficiary of the dollar's weakness against the euro and the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to 44.52 baht per dollar, compared with THB44.745 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After riding on the coattails of the yen's gains earlier in the day, the New Taiwan dollar lost ground as it yielded to foreign equity fund outflows, which contributed to a 1.7% slide on the local bourse, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$34.603, higher than NT$34.586 Tuesday. The U.S. dollar had touched an intraday low of NT$34.496. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over Taiwan's sluggish economic fundamentals will likely continue to weigh on the local dollar, observers said. \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Economic Affairs said Wednesday Taiwan's July export orders sank 16.8% to US$10.8 billion, sharper than the average 14.3% year-on-year drop the market had expected. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's July industrial output fell 10.4%, compared with a 11.3% decline in June. The data was mostly in line with expectations of a 10.1% drop. \par \par \uc2  Renewed fears of a further increase in local banks' reserve requirements shored up the Philippine peso, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Somewhat alleviating the market's jitters, Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Amando Tetangco said later in the day an increase in banks' reserve requirement was only "an option" for draining excess liquidity from the financial system, should it be causing inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 50.91 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, down from PHP51.25 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have bought dollars as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated, to replenish its reserves and smooth the peso's volatility, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, corporate dollar demand weighed on the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which slipped to 8,650 rupiah to the dollar, compared with IDR8,545 late Tuesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4043; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010822dx8m000ga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
