\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Korea finmin sees only rare  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2001\par 09:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, April 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Jin Nyum said on Thursday the government would not step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the won unless it saw panic or speculation. \par \par \uc2  "I acknowledge there was lack of communication between the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea over the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy last week," the minister said. \par \par \uc2  "As for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, we will coordinate with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the minister. \par \par \uc2  "We may take action and use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to stabilise the market. But the authorities' action will be limited to such cases as investors are believed to be in a panic about the rate or any speculative movements are seen," Jin added. \par \par \uc2  As the won continued to weaken against the U.S. dollar following the movement of the Japanese yen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had said last week it would use \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, if needed, to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The won, which closely tracks the yen, is down about five percent against the dollar this year after falling about 11 percent last year. \par \par \uc2  The won fell to 30-month lows last week, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea to buy dollars to stem its slide. \par \par \uc2  SEOULBANK SALE \par \par \uc2  Jin, speaking in a news conference, also said the sales of Seoulbank and Hyundai Investment Trust would be completed by the end of June. \par \par \uc2  Seoulbank said last month 18 foreign investors, mostly from the United States, had shown an interest in taking over the nationalised bank and it expected the sale to be completed in the first half. \par \par \uc2  The government, the largest single shareholder of Seoulbank, had pumped 4.8 trillion won ($3.64 billion) in public money into the bank between 1998 and 1999, to help it find a buyer. \par \par \uc2  The government had said it would put Seoulbank under a state-run financial holding company if it was not sold. \par \par \uc2  Hyundai has said for months it was close to concluding a deal to draw nearly $1 billion in investment from a consortium led by American International Group (AIG) and WL Ross. \par \par \uc2  The consortium and the Hyundai companies signed a memorandum of understanding on the planned investment last August. \par \par \uc2  But the sale of ailing Korea Life Insurance is not likely to be completed by June as the government has to select lead managers, Jin said. \par \par \uc2  The government had earlier said it would sell the unlisted life insurer within this year after injecting 1.5 trillion won during the first half of this year to facilitate the sale. \par \par \uc2  State-run Korea Deposit Insurance Corp has injected 2.05 trillion won and owns a 100 percent stake in Korea, which was declared non-viable in September 1999 by the financial regulator. \par \par \uc2  ($1=1320.0 Won). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4c026s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
