\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Won Ends Lower, Tracks Weaker Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 09:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won finished significantly weaker against the dollar as the Japanese yen slipped despite a surge in the Nikkei. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,305.3 won compared with Tuesday's close of KRW1,295.9. The won rose in early trade as Korea Development Bank (Q.KDV) sold dollars to support a strong won opening. However, the dollar later surged and hit an intraday high of KRW1,306. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei's 7.5% jump, spurred by the Japanese government's public fund buying, briefly strengthened the yen, and in turn the won, but this wasn't sustained with the yen retreating below Y123 against the dollar. The won closely tracks the yen and has been steadily weakening over the past month. On Feb. 21, the won closed at KRW1,238 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A trader said the won will likely trade between KRW1,300 to KRW1,308 to the dollar Thursday. \par \par \uc2  An official at the Bank of Korea told Dow Jones Newswires that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "cannot exclude the possibility" of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the weakening won. "When it is time, we will do it," the official said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said it was closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market trends and that it was worried about "excessive jitters" among participants. However, the verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to push the won higher against the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond yields rose Wednesday on the weakening won, disappointment over the size of the U.S. interest rate cut and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's remarks in the morning. The U.S. Federal Open Market Committee cut interest rates by 50 basis points Tuesday, but many market players in Korea had been hoping for a 75 basis point cut. \par \par \uc2  The yield on benchmark three-year government bonds rose eight basis points to 5.71% and the yield on five-year government bonds rose 12 basis points to 6.34%. The one-year government bond yield rose eight basis points to 5.80%. \par \par \uc2  The two-year monetary stabilization bond yield jumped 15 basis points to 5.97% and the one-year monetary stabilization rose seven basis points to 5.81%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on three-year corporate bonds with a rating of AA- rose 10 basis points to 7.17% and the yield on three-year corporate bonds with a rating of BBB- rose 11 basis points to 12.06%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on three-month certificates of deposit rose one basis point to 5.68%. \par \par \uc2  The one-day call rate slipped two basis points to 4.97%. \par \par \uc2  As reported, Bank of Korea Governor Chon Chol-hwan said consumer prices could rise more than 4% in the first half of this year if the won continues to fall rapidly against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, state-run Korea Deposit Insurance Corp. sold KRW1.54 trillion worth of five-year bonds at a yield of 7.29%. Market participants had expected KDIC to sell around KRW1 trillion of bonds at 7.30%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Seoul Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-732-2156\par -0- 21/03/01 08-50G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3l017de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
