\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won Slides To 25-Month Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2000\par 10:37\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won fell to a more than two-year low against the dollar Tuesday in holiday-thinned trade, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished regional trading hours mixed. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and Philippine peso strengthened against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah weakened. The New Taiwan dollar was little changed. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, even modest gains in the local stock market couldn't prop up the embattled won, which has lost 3.8% of its value in the past week and more than 9% since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said ongoing concerns over South Korea's corporate and financial sector restructuring, as well as a prolonged labor strike at two major banks, kept the dollar well-bid against the won. \par \par \uc2  Two of Asia's leading \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Australia and Hong Kong, were closed Tuesday in observance of Boxing Day. In addition, many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers elsewhere in Asia took the day off following Monday's Christmas holiday and in advance of the Eid al-Fitr celebration that begins Tuesday evening. \par \par \uc2  Against the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar closed at 1,254 won, its strongest finish since Nov. 20, 1998, when the dollar ended at KRW1,263. The dollar closed at KRW1,237 Friday. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. bank said while the sharp drop in the won surprised many market participants, there was still strong dollar demand when trading finished for the day. \par \par \uc2  "We're looking at 1,280 (won) as the next resistance level for the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  While movements in the won are sometimes driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, this time it appeared to be "genuine" dollar demand at work he said. However, the movement may have been somewhat exaggerated due to the thin market conditions, he added. \par \par \uc2  There was verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy in the morning and late dollar selling from state-run Korea Development Bank and Export-Import Bank of Korea, but the moves weren't sufficient to check the dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  In Taipei, the New Taiwan dollar overcame a 1.9% decline in the local stock market to finish virtually unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.179, compared with Friday's close of NT$33.180, a 22-month low for the local dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Offshore U.S. dollar-buying pressured the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower as it\par fell from initial highs near NT$33.150 and as the local stock market\par fell to near five-year lows, dealers said.\par - - 26/12/00 09-37G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar remained well-bid against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead\par of Wednesday's market-closing Eid al-Fitr holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late in Asian trading the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7250, down\par from S$1.7275 late Friday. The dollar fell as low as S$1.7220 in\par intraday trading, close to its 15-week low of S$1.7217 reached in\par intraday trading Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar benefitted from the continued strength of the\par euro and the island state's strong economic fundamentals, a dealer at an\par Asian bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso was boosted by market-positive developments on the\par political front, slack corporate demand for U.S. dollars and remittance\par inflows from overseas workers, traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at 49.420 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System,\par down from PHP49.900 Friday, but off its intraday low of PHP49.090.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A bank executive's potentially damaging testimony against President\par Joseph Estrada at the impeachment trial last Friday raised hopes among\par market participants that the country's political crisis may be edging\par towards a resolution.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupiah weakened in offshore trading Tuesday after market sentiment\par toward the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was hit by a series of Christmas Eve bombings at\par Christian churches across Indonesia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at 9,420 rupiah, up from IDR9,365\par late Friday. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets were closed in Indonesia for a\par holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer said pressure on the rupiah will\par continue amid expectations anti-Christian violence will continue as the\par Muslim Eid al-Fitr holiday continues this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted at 42.115 baht in late\par Asian trading, up from THB42.050 late Monday in thin trade. The Bangkok\par market was one of the few in Asia open for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading\par Monday during the Christmas holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 