\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won, New Taiwan Dlr Lower \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2000\par 10:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar took a hit Thursday from falling stock prices and fears of slower economic growth, while most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened in thin yearend trading. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht, Singapore dollar and Philippine peso all gained on the U.S. dollar, while the Indonesian rupiah weakened. \par \par \uc2  Share prices in Seoul, Taiwan and Tokyo tumbled on the heels of a sharp 7.1% overnight decline in the beleaguered U.S. Nasdaq market. \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy failed to halt the slide of the won, which closed at a 21-month low. So far this year the won has lost 7.5% of its value against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading the dollar was quoted at 1,227.9 won, up from KRW1,217.0 late Wednesday. It was the dollar's strongest finish since March 15, 1999, when it closed at KRW1,230.9. \par \par \uc2  Korean officials issued a brief statement saying they were monitoring market developments and would take "proper measures when necessary." But no actual \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reported. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said expectations the U.S. dollar will continue to strengthen into the new year added to the won's decline. Sentiment toward the won also was damped by a planned strike Friday by workers at six ailing South Korean banks that are the targets of a government-led restructuring program. \par \par \uc2  Korea's over-the-counter Kosdaq , heavily laden with high-tech shares, dropped 5% to a record low close of 56.06 points. \par \par \uc2  An economic slowdown underway in the U.S. has taken a bite out of profits of formerly high-flying high technology and telecommunications companies. The North Asian economies, which are highly dependent on electronics exports to the U.S., have suffered as a result of the U.S. downturn. \par \par \uc2  A poor stock market performance and weakness in the yen combined to push the New Taiwan dollar to a 22-month low. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.172 - the strongest finish since NT$33.257 on Feb. 20, 1999. The dollar closed Wednesday at NT$33.129. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold an estimated $300 million in an attempt\par to control the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decline, dealers said.\par - - 21/12/00 09-48G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai baht closed at a 10-week high against the dollar after\par exporters bought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to balance their books by yearend, a\par dealer at an Asian bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at 42.425 baht, down from THB42.590 late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar followed the Thai baht higher for most of the day\par until the rise apparently caught the eye of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par Singapore.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Several dealers said they suspected the MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market\par near the end of Asian trading to prevent "excessive appreciation" of the\par Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after it hit a 15-week high against the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have weakened recently against the\par U.S. dollar, the Singapore dollar has been strengthening. This concerns\par the MAS because it erodes Singapore's competitiveness in export markets\par in which it competes with other Asian countries.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It is the MAS' policy not to comment on its market activities. But the\par trading pattern in the Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday bore\par all the earmarks MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a dealer at a U.S. bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They're very good at what they do," the dealer said of the MAS. "They\par let the market get a little short and then they start buying dollars."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar hit a low of S$1.7270 in intraday trading, which apparently\par is at the bottom end of their "tolerance level," the dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MAS apparently began buying U.S. dollars at that level and pushed\par the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to an intraday high of S$1.7332 within 30 minutes. The\par U.S. dollar eased slightly by the close to finish at $1.7300, down from\par $1.7345 late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso strengthened due to a combination of dollar\par remittances from workers overseas and weak corporate demand for the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar closed at 50.045 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System,\par down from PHP50.058 Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah weakened despite a round of dollar-selling by\par state banks designed to check the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After trading between 9,230 rupiah and IDR9,400, the dollar closed at\par IDR9,295, up from IDR9,240 Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421-4859;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par -0- 21/12/00 10-47G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwcl063oi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
