\par\par \b \uc2  Korea won ends weaker,foreign stock selling weighs. \b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2000\par 10:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Dec 4 (Reuters) - The South Korean won -2 closed weaker on Monday, depressed by foreign investors' settlement of local stock sales. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said won sentiment was bleak as investors remained downcast over the crawling pace of government-led restructuring and signs of a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  The won finished at 1,217.1 per dollar, a fresh closing low for the year and its lowest ebb since September 29, 1999. It had opened at 1,203.0 from Friday's close of 1,209.5. \par \par \uc2  Fears of government-backed dollar sales by state banks kept the market on its toes, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  withered lower as oil companies and offshore players continued to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  "Right now the focus is on how serious the BOK (\b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea) is about direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how much foreign investors will sell in the stock market," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were spooked by state-owned Korea Development Bank's (KDB) aggressive dollar selling on Friday as they saw the move as a government-backed effort to defend the 1,220 level. \par \par \uc2  KDB sold another $50-$100 million on Monday, but volumes were weak and the market took the seemingly passive stance as a green light to break the 1,215 support level. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the government was unlikely as \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  was loathe to use up its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Analysts also saw the authorities tolerating a weaker won to boost exports. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Jin Nyum said counter-measures would be taken against speculative trade in the non-deliverable forward market and any sharp fluctuation in the dollar/won rate, but did not specify what kind of measures would be implemented. \par \par \uc2  Disappointment about the pace of reforms and slowing economic growth have also plagued the local stock market, which is off 50 percent for the year. The benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) ended down 2.47 percent at 501.73. \par \par \uc2  Investors quickly turned cool on parliament's approval on Saturday of 40 trillion in additional funds to recapitalise financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net sellers of 34.2 billion won worth of stocks on Monday after selling a net 400 billion won worth last Thursday and Friday. Though only part of the amount was expected in the market, dealers said settlement of the stock sales would continue to pressure the won. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected a near-term range of 1,212-22. \par \par \uc2  International Monetary Fund senior representative in Seoul David Coe said South Korea risked seeing another banking crisis if local banks continued to support nonviable companies. \par \par \uc2  Coe also said grouping weak banks under financial holding companies may not help, but saw the probability of a crisis like the one in 1997-98 as very low. \par \par \uc2  On the non-deliverable forward market, the six-month won was last quoted at 1,216.7/17.7 from 1,212.8/13.8 earlier, while the one-year won was last at 1,216.9/17.9 from 1,213.5/14.5. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010823dwc400tmr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
