\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won, Taiwan Dlr Extend Falls \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2000\par 10:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar extended their falls late Monday on domestic and international pressures, while other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged a mixed performance. \par \par \uc2  "It's pretty much an established downtrend for the Taiwan dollar and the Korean won," said Chi Lo, a senior economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The weakness of these Northeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  wiped out the Singapore dollar's earlier gains, which were fueled by the Japanese yen's strong performance and the euro's recent boost, dealers said. The Singapore dollar slipped back below S$1.7500 support late in the day. \par \par \uc2  Continued remittance inflows from Filipinos working overseas lifted the Philippine peso, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Offshore participants showed sparse interest in the Thai baht and the Indonesian rupiah, both of which were steady. \par \par \uc2  Foreign equity fund outflows pressured the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Taiwan, although \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day cushioned the impact on the latter, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 1,216.70 won, up from Friday's close of KRW1,209.50. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a 20-month high of NT$33.071, from the previous close of NT$32.995, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  withdrew its support for the local dollar late in the day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over domestic political bickering in Taiwan and uncertainties over South Korea's progress toward economic reform continued to cloud the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of both nations, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The risk of both economies slipping into a current account deficit next year, as a result of an expected slowdown in exports, could also harm their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Lo at Standard Chartered Bank. The semiconductor cycle is turning down and personal computer sales are slowing, he added. The authorities should also allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken to keep their countries' exports competitive, with the Japanese yen expected to lose ground, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But the pressure on the won will likely be more intense, with the Taiwanese authorities continuing to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the pace of the local dollar's depreciation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "For those investors who don't like the short-term prospects and want to short these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they could use the Korean won as a proxy to the Taiwan dollar," said Lo at Standard Chartered Bank, adding that this could add to the pressure on the won. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's Minister of Finance and Economy Jin Nyum, said Monday the government isn't considering any direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the won's losses. He reiterated, however, that the government will closely monitor trading in the non-deliverable forward market. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. dollar rebounded to S$1.7525, after pulling back to as low as S$1.7475 earlier in the day. Late Friday, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7521. \par \par \uc2  While the Singapore dollar's early rebound had prompted some dealers to say the U.S. dollar may have found a short-term top at around S$1.7600, UBS Warburg's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, Mansoor Mohi-uddin, said the Singapore dollar remains vulnerable to potential further weakness in the yen. \par \par \uc2  In the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar edged up slightly to 43.850 baht, from THB43.790 late Friday. The baht was largely steady earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The banning of same-day cash sales between onshore and offshore counterparties without any commercial purpose has made the market more cautious of shorting the baht for now, noted UBS Warburg's Mohi-uddin. \par \par \uc2  But he added that the baht's respite will likely be transient amid political uncertainties ahead of Thailand's general elections early next year. \par \par \uc2  "We still have interest from some of our clients to short the baht ahead of elections," Mohi-uddin said, adding that the authorities won't tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations further for now, after clamping down on speculative trading last week. \par \par \uc2  "The market is waiting for the liquidity squeeze to ease, and then they'll start buying dollars," he said. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was steady at 9,525 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the dollar edged up to 49.470 pesos, from Friday's close of PHP49.460. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwc405upn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
