\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  dealers in Korea halt speculative trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kim Myong-hwan \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2000\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Aug 25 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers at major banks in South Korea, including foreign banks, have halted speculative trading to protest against government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move which would severely curtail liquidity, industry sources said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The government's tight control on the market has frustrated dealers because the subsequent narrow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range seriously limits their ability to make proprietary trading profits for their banks. \par \par \uc2  Halting speculative trade would erase about 80 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's volume and although the traders admit it would be largely symbolic, it is important for a country to have as liquid a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as possible to facilitate overseas trade and investment flows. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea official said the impact was seen on Thursday as trade fell to about $1.2 billion from the recent daily average of $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  Trade by late Friday was estimated at a scant $370 million but the won's level was little changed as it hovered at Thursday's closing of 1,114.1 at 0617 GMT. \par \par \uc2  It opened at 1,113.9 and moved between 1,113.9 and 1,114.4. \par \par \uc2  "Our action is a symbolic gesture," said a foreign bank dealer. "We are of an opinion that the government has been behind the current boring, tight range trade." \par \par \uc2  The won has closed between 1,110 and 1,119 every day for the last two months. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a local bank expressed a similar view: "The government's excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stifles the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. It also severely undermines corporate awareness about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said recent trade had been held to a tight range by what they dubbed "government-instructed" importer and exporter settlements. \par \par \uc2  About 30 dealers met on Thursday and agreed to stop speculative trading, limiting themselves to commercial orders. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the agreement would hold until at least early next week, while urging the government to allow market principles to prevail. \par \par \uc2  SMOOTHING \b \highlight22\uc2 OPERATIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Monetary authorities deny they are overstepping their mandate, which is basically to ensure that the won, buoyed by the economy's robust rebound this year and last, does not appreciate too fast. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the International Monetary Fund said \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". \par \par \uc2  Authorities also denied they were influencing the market by directing action by state-run banks or other state agencies. \par \par \uc2  "Dealers seem to have taken this action because the current tight range of trade means little opportunity for profits," said Joo Hwan-wook, deputy director of the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy division. "We are not doing the things the dealers accuse us of." \par \par \uc2  WON UNDERVALUED VS DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the won was undervalued given dollar inflows of foreign equity investors and the country's trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have bought a net $10 billion worth of Korean stocks so far this year and trade surplus by July this year was $5 billion, government figures showed. \par \par \uc2  "The won's fair value should be between 1,050 and 1,110 per dollar," said Namuh Rhee, executive director at Samsung Securities. \par \par \uc2  Kevin Ralph, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Sanwa Bank in Singapore, said South Korean monetary authorities had long stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  "directly". \par \par \uc2  "But they are still believed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through state-run and banks and companies," Ralph said. "I believe the won is currently about three to five percent undervalued against the dollar." \par \par \uc2  South Korea has adopted a floating exchange rate system since the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, when the won plummeted from about 913 to the dollar in October 1997 to a close of 1,806 won three months later. \par \par \uc2  "In textbook theory, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  should see much less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government," said an economist at the state-funded Korea Development Institute. \par \par \uc2  "But our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is too small for the economy. So the government still needs to step in to maintain the market's stability," he said. \par \par \uc2  The won has appreciated about 1.8 percent since the close of 1999. \par \par \uc2  ($1=1,114.20 won). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8p0a0jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
