\par\par \b \uc2  Korea won ends lower on corporate, bank dlr demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2000\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SEOUL, Feb 24 (Reuters) - The South Korean won\tab     -2 ended weaker against the greenback on Thursday on dollar demand from importers and banks settling contracts for foreign investors' recent sales of local stocks, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The won closed at 1,140.5 compared with Wednesday's close of 1,135.3.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  It opened at 1,137.0 and dropped to 1,141.4 in late morning trade on the back of the dollar's bullishness versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As the dollar/yen was well-bid at around 111 yen, there was little dollar-selling interest spotted in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market saw an inflow of more than $70 million from the recent sales of local stocks by foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  Oil refiners and other importers also bought dollars estimated at $50 million. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar/won was pushed back to the 1,137 won level in afternoon trade as players eased positions after the IMF's departing managing director for Asia-Pacific Hubert Neiss said Asian nations should not resist \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation pressures. \par \par \uc2  Neiss told a conference in Hong Kong that large current account surpluses were inevitably putting upward pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it was useless to try to target any specific exchange rate level. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities have frequently said they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with "smoothing out" \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar/won rate became too volatile. \par \par \uc2  The dollar regained ground and went back to the 1,140 won level later in the day as the monetary authorities stepped in, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea said on Thursday South Korea's provisional current account surplus for 1999 stood at $25 billion, down from $40.56 billion in 1998. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attributed the falls to increased demand for imports, spurred by the recovering domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. unit could come under pressure on exporter offers toward the end of the month if the dollar/yen pair remained stable. \par \par \uc2  The won is expected to move between 1,137 and 1,145 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The six-month non-deliverable forward (NDF) won was quoted at 1,140/41 at 0700 GMT versus 1,136/37 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The one-year won stood at 1,144/45 against 1,139/41. \par \par \uc2  ((Nam In-soo +82 2 727-5644, fax +82 2 727-5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw2o06obx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
