\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW - S.Korea bond fund says to cap yields. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Min Byung-bok \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 1999\par 03:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Nov 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Bond Market Stabilisation Fund will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local debt market aiming to prevent benchmark three-year bond yields from rising from current levels, a senior fund official said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The fund will ensure that the three-year government and corporate bond yields do not rise from current levels before the year's end," the fund's chief manager Paik Kyung-ho told Reuters in a telephone interview. \par \par \uc2  Paik also said he expected bond yields to begin drifting lower next year if the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation against the dollar continues and if international oil prices decline. \par \par \uc2  "We are expecting the market to turn bullish next year with the market's long-term interest rates declining," he said. \par \par \uc2  Paik's comments come less than a week after the stabilisation fund \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Wednesday to push down the benchmark three-year treasury bond yield to 8.60 percent from 9.04 percent. \par \par \uc2  Bond yields had steadily risen as investors shunned the longer-dated three-year issues on expectations the country's rapid economic recovery will lead to inflationary pressure next year. \par \par \uc2  Government officials led by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy have played down the threat of inflationary pressure rising next year and said they would take measures to ensure long-term interest rates stay stable. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over the direction of long-term rates and the fear of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the stabilisation fund have nearly paralysed the local bond market, locking the benchmark yield at 8.60 percent since the fund \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Market players have also said the stabilisation fund's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suppression of the benchmark three-year yield has distorted the country's yield curve with the three-year yield falling below those of one-and two-year assets. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, the benchmark yield closed at 8.60 percent while the the 364-day issue closed at 8.65 percent and two-year Monetary Stabilisation Bonds ended at 9.01 percent. \par \par \uc2  But Paik said the fund's role was to stabilise the benchmark yield and that other market players should take on the responsibility of resolving the distorted yield curve. \par \par \uc2  ((Seoul Newsroom, +82 2 727 5642, fax +82 2 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbt09652\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
