\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS - Korea under fire over won  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kim Kyoung-Wha \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1999\par 03:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The South Korean government is under fire over its efforts to sap the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it tries to steer a course toward an export-led recovery while heading off inflationary pressures, analysts said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea insists it is just smoothing out volatile trade and is not trying to dictate market trends. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the case, the irrepressible won was back on its robust course Tuesday, closing at 1,177.8 compared with Monday's close of 1,182, despite heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Its strength is largely a result of foreign investors piling into the stock market, analysts said. Some $1.5 billion has flowed into the market so far this month from abroad. \par \par \uc2  WON SEEN UNDERVALUED WITH INFLATION \par \par \uc2  Many analysts say the won is undervalued given the current strength of the economic recovery and potential inflationary pressures building up in the economy. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Korea must let the won go higher to absorb inflationary pressure that is expected to intensify next year," said an economist at Samsung Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  But Hannuri Securities economist Lee Chong-won said the government wanted a weaker won to achieve its $25-billion trade surplus target for this year. In January to October, South Korea posted a $19 billion trade surplus year-on-year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks clumsy to some. \par \par \uc2  "The government seems to be forgetting hard lessons that it learned over the financial crisis and returning to old practices of attempting to put the exchange rate under its control," said an economist at LG Economic Research. \par \par \uc2  RESERVES HAVE RISEN 10 TIMES \par \par \uc2  Two years ago, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  squandered its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in a futile effort to prop up the downward spiralling won during the "Asian Contagion" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  Seoul was forced to turn to the International Monetary Fund for a $58.35 billion bail-out when its reserves dipped to below $6 billion in early December 1997. \par \par \uc2  But now reserves have risen by more than 10 times to $68.4 billion as of November 15. The finance ministry said on Tuesday reserves might top $70 billion in November. Dealers construed that to mean the authorities plan to stay in the market. \par \par \uc2  Extensive dollar purchases by state banks, ranging between $100 million and $300 million per day this month, are the main factor shoring up the dollar for now, dealers analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The government is taking a multi-pronged approach to beating down the won. It will issue 5 trillion ($4.2 billion) in won-denominated bonds this month, the proceeds of which will be used to buy dollars. It is also moving forward a year-end deadline for banks to provision for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan losses. \par \par \uc2  NOT A FULLY CONVERTIBLE \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the government was just trying to keep an embryonic market on an even keel. The won is not a fully convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market is not that liquid. \par \par \uc2  "Since the local market has not fully ripened, authorities need to meddle to help the market find an equilibrium in demand and supply," said the official. \par \par \uc2  Dollar demand will pick up in the next few months because banks will need $4 billion to pay back foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans due by April 15, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Domestic financial institutions also have to set aside about $1.4-1.5 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loan loss reserves against possible losses to the Daewoo Group, he added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  by instructing the Bank of Korea as well as state-run banks, including Korea Development Bank and Export-Import Bank of Korea, to purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank must tell the IMF when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  under their agreement. So far, the IMF has not objected, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Steve Xu, regional economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong, said the government should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It might work today or tomorrow or next week. It's not going to work in the end," he said. \par \par \uc2  "With the election coming up, the government seems preoccupied with sustaining exports and economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  South Korea will hold a general election in April. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 1,177 won) \par \par \uc2  (Seoul Newsroom +822-727-5647, fax +822-727-5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dvbh09y3p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
