\par\par \b \uc2  Asian govts watch nervously as  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  climb. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1999\par 05:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 2 (Reuters) - Following Asia's bitter two-year experience with slumping \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ballooning overseas debt, governments in the region should now be breathing a sigh of relief that exchange rates are improving. \par \par \uc2  But the upturn in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is worrying regional governments anxious to protect fledgling export recoveries. \par \par \uc2  Central banks hold little hope of turning back the tide of cash flooding into their markets. The most they can aim to do is merely slow the flow. \par \par \uc2  Japan is the most blatant case of a government finding it impossible to stand aside and watch a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  savage export earnings. \par \par \uc2  Its repeated dollar buying and verbal onslaughts since the release of unexpectedly strong first quarter data have kept the yen from firming beyond 118.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The central banks of Korea and Taiwan - Japan's major export rivals - have also been spotted buying dollars in the market to counter sharp rises in Korea's won and the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  While Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry have denied wanting to manipulate the exchange rate to benefit exporters, the government has halved its 1999 export growth forecast to 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  And with Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi saying unchecked rallies in the baht could hurt exports, many dealers suspect Thailand may seek to keep a lid on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts say regional central banks are unlikely to do anything more than merely slow the pace of appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of Asian policymakers still remember the result of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulations in the past. Central banks are so scared now that they are really committed to a more flexible exchange rate regime," said Quah Hong Chye, strategist at HypoVereinsbank. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they are trying to manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to alter the direction. They are just \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the speed and volatility of the movement," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea has been less than coy about its intentions, frequently expressing disapproval with the won's sharp rise and buying dollars through state banks as well as directly in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  But with the economy growing an unexpectedly robust 4.6 percent year-on-year in the first quarter and the stock market revisiting mid-1996 highs, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to live with a stronger won. \par \par \uc2  "The Korean economy is fundamentally recovering so they have to let it appreciate. But had they not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we would have been at 1,150 (won per dollar) months ago," said Quah. \par \par \uc2  The won was trading at around 1,167 to the dollar on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund, which arranged a near $60 billion bailout package for \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late 1997, has endorsed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as aimed at smoothing moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not weakening it. \par \par \uc2  Despite all the official fretting, Korean exports reached a record monthly high of $12.98 billion in June, suggesting the won's strength is not putting too much strain on trade. \par \par \uc2  "There are renewed inflows coming back into the country and to have that stall the export recovery is certainly the last thing they would want to see," said an analyst at a U.S. investment house. \par \par \uc2  "But on the other hand, there isn't really a country whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is appreciating in isolation so there isn't a loss of relative competitiveness," he said. \par \par \uc2  Much of the caution about a premature \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation is rooted in the knowledge that a major external shock could still trip up the Asian recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Things are improving certainly, but it doesn't mean they couldn't get worse if the U.S. goes in for a hard landing, or if the Japanese economy doesn't pick up as people expect," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "So all things considered, it's probably a fair policy to expect them to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable in this economic environment," she added. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv720586x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
