\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Drift Lower; Mkts Wind Dn At Month-End \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1999\par 10:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted lower against the U.S. dollar in thin end of the month trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Activity was dominated by position adjustments as regional markets entered what one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker in Singapore described as the "twighlight zone" ahead of the end of the half-year and an expected increase in U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Despite the fall, market sentiment toward regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains broadly positive. Traders and investors alike continue to strongly favor the South Korean won, whose drop Tuesday was impelled largely by U.S. dollar purchases said to have been executed on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea in recent sessions have successfully supported the U.S. dollar above KRW1,150, dealers and analysts agree that it is only a matter of time before the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  renews its slide. \par \par \uc2  "The market trend is clear, said Lian Chia Liang," \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate analyst at Merrill Lynch in Singapore. "Over the course of the second half of the year the won will move towards KRW1,100" against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  According to Lian, real effective exchange rate calculations indicate that a fair value for the won is currently around KRW900 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although he does not believe the nominal exchange rate will approach that level in the short- to medium-term, Lian says that powerful underlying forces will continue to strengthen the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite the government's continued efforts to absorb dollars from the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Government policy may slow the pace of the won's appreciation somewhat, but I doubt very much if we will see the won back at KRW1,200" to the dollar, Lian said. \par \par \uc2  At the end of local dealings Tuesday, the U.S. dollar closed at KRW1,157.00, up from KRW1,156.20 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 29-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0830GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6t04wdq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
