\par\par \b \uc2  S. Korea's Ctrl Bk Buys Dlrs -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Strong \b0\par\par\uc2 175 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 03:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Korea, is buying the dollar at 1,161 won early Wednesday through commerical banks as part of its efforts to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the dollar rose to KRW1,167 briefly, but edged down to KRW1,166.50 at 0045 GMT (8:45 p.m. Tuesday EDT), higher than Wedneday's opening at KRW1,160 and KRW1,159.40 at the close of trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Right after Wednesday's opening, state-run Export-Import Bank of Korea, and Korea Development Bank were seen buying the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying is pretty strong, but local exporters are using the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell their dollar holdings, preventing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising further," said a trader at Housing and Commericial Bank of Korea. \par \par \uc2  -By Chang Woo-hyuk; 822-732-2165; woo-hyuk.chang@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 23-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0106GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6n04pxa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
