\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Korea steps in again to slow  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 's rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yeom Yoon-jeong \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 11:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, June 23 (Reuters) - South Korea's finance minister said the government would act to rein in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar, but his comments and two direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday had little impact on the won. \par \par \uc2  "The government won't directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet a targeted rate," Kang Bong-kyun told a breakfast meeting. "But the government will try to fulfil its role (to stabilise the market)." \par \par \uc2  He did not say whether the government had a targeted won/dollar rate or what an appropriate rate might be. \par \par \uc2  A state-run bank dealer said the government was intensifying its efforts to slow the rise of the won because it was just 10 won from what was viewed as the government's ideal rate. \par \par \uc2  "We have witnessed strong \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January and this month when the dollar neared 1,150 won," he said. \par \par \uc2  The won stood at 1,162.8 against the dollar when local trading closed on Wednesday compared with Tuesday's 1,159.4. \par \par \uc2  From the opening bell on Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally and physically in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea bought about $350 million, including a $50 million purchase, before the close. \par \par \uc2  Two senior finance ministry officials said the government would act to stop the won's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "The government will take effective measures to defend the dollar against the won," said Kim Yong-duk, director-general of the international finance bureau at the ministry. \par \par \uc2  But the series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not reverse the trend of the won substantially. \par \par \uc2  "Market players have already tested the government's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  which were powerless," said Lee Chang-young, a dealer at Kookmin Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We do not see the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as having any lasting effect as market sentiment remains very bullish on the Korean won," Catherine Tan, managing analyst for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Thomson Global Markets, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "I think eventually the government is not going to win the fight against the market sentiment due to the huge influx of funds," she said. \par \par \uc2  The won is still 27 percent weaker than its pre-crisis level of 850 won per dollar, but South Korea is concerned that a strong won will hurt exports and stall its nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger won makes it difficult for Korean exporters to compete internationally, especially against Japan for value-added products and Southeast Asia for cheap goods. \par \par \uc2  Kang said the government needed to control the dollar supply later this year by boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, a senior finance ministry official said South Korea might have to increase the amount of won-denominated bonds in the second half of this year to cap the won's rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the government was also concerned about the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, mainly borrowings, and the possibility of foreign equity funds flowing out if the stock market became bearish. \par \par \uc2  Kang said the government might use the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in overseas investment and in export financing. \par \par \uc2  Jung Woon-gab, dealer at Arab Bank, said: "I'm not quite sure whether the government's plan will work out. My question is what the government plans to do in the short-term, like in June." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6n06074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
