\par\par \b \uc2  SKorea moves again to slow won appreciation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yeom Yoon-jeong \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1999\par 05:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, June 23 (Reuters) - South Korea's finance minister said on Wednesday the government would take steps to rein in the the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar but his comments and a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little impact on the won. \par \par \uc2  "The government won't directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet a targeted rate. But the government will try to fulfill its role (to stabilise the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)," Kang Bong-kyun said in a breakfast meeting. \par \par \uc2  He did not say whether the government had a targeted won/dollar rate or what an appropriate rate might be. \par \par \uc2  A state-run bank dealer said the government was intensifying its efforts to slow the rise of the won because it was just 10 won from what was viewed as the government's bottom line. \par \par \uc2  "We have witnessed strong \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in January and this month, when the dollar nears 1,150 won," he said. \par \par \uc2  The won closed at midday on Wednesday at 1,163.5, against Tuesday's close of 1,159.4. \par \par \uc2  From the opening bell on Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally and physically in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea (BOK) bought an estimated $300 million and two senior finance ministry officials said the government would act to stop the won's appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "It is undesirable for the won to appreciate (against the dollar) when the yen continues to depreciate (versus the dollar)," said Yun Yong-ro, a director at the ministry. \par \par \uc2  The dollar remained stable at around 122 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The government will take effective measures to defend the dollar against the won," said Kim Yong-duk, director-general of the international finance bureau at the ministry. \par \par \uc2  But the series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not substantially reverse the trend of the won, and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up just three won one hour later. \par \par \uc2  "Market players have already tested the government's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which are powerless," said Lee Chang-young, a dealer at Kookmin Bank. \par \par \uc2  "The market will hardly move with indirect (verbal) \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the government has resorted to that too often," said Jung In-woo, a dealer at ABN-Amro Bank. \par \par \uc2  Even though the won is still 27 percent weaker than its pre-crisis level of 850 won per dollar, South Korea, a major exporter, is concerned its appreciation will hurt its export competitiveness and stall its nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger won makes it difficult for the country's exporters to compete in international markets, especially against Japan in value-added products and southeast Asian countries in cheap products. \par \par \uc2  The minister said: "The government is needed to control dollar supply in the second half including by boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand." \par \par \uc2  His comment came one day after a senior finance ministry official said South Korea may increase the amount of won-denominated bonds to be issued during the second half of this year to cap the won's rise against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the government was also concerned about the nature of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which were largely borrowings, and about the inflow of foreign equity funds which could leave suddenly if the stock market became bearish. \par \par \uc2  Kang said the government might use the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in overseas investment and in export financing such as tied-loans. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not quite sure whether the government plan will work out. My question is what the government plans to do in the short-term, like in June," said Jung Woon-gab, a dealer at Arab Bank. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6n05zmd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
