\par\par \b \uc2  S. Korea Won Up Early; Foreign Interest In Rights Issues \b0\par\par\uc2 242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 03:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won is up early Tuesday, as modest dollar-buying by a state-run bank isn't sufficient to offset won-buying by foreign investors keen on recent rights issues by Korean companies. \par \par \uc2  Around 0106 GMT (9:06 p.m. Monday EDT), the dollar was trading at 1,164.30 won, down from Monday's closing level of KRW1,165.50. \par \par \uc2  State-run Export-Import Bank of Korea was seen buying dollars early Tuesday around KRW1,165, but the bank's dollar purchases weren't sufficient to support that level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The won is being supported by dollar-selling by foreign investors entering the local stock market. While foreigners have been net sellers of local stocks in recent trading sessions, many of those investors haven't converted their won proceeds into dollars. \par \par \uc2  Instead, they have been buying additional won to purchase rights shares of local companies, such as Samsung Electronics Co. (Q.SSE). \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the dollar to trade Tuesday between KRW1,160 and KRW1,166. \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Korea, which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market several times during the last two weeks, is expected to remain on the sidelines Tuesday unless the dollar slips below KRW1,160. \par \par \uc2  -By Louis Hau; 822-732-2165; lhau@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0129GMT \par \par \uc2  1063 1858 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6m04qwi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict