\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-S.Korea c.bank sees 5 pct '99 GDP growth. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bill Tarrant \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 1999\par 10:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, June 14 (Reuters) - South Korea's 1999 gross domestic product growth should exceed five percent, and with low inflation and plenty of slack in the economy, that pace can be sustained for a while, Bank of Korea chief Chon Chol-hwan said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Private consumption and investment in factory expansion and equipment have risen sharply this year, and exports have begun to pick up, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "Even so, inflation remains very low and there's still a lot of slack in the economy," Chon said. "This means the recovery of business activity now under way should continue for some time." \par \par \uc2  "These factors will act to push up GDP growth to a higher level than in the first quarter, so we are now looking at somewhere above five percent for the whole year," he said. \par \par \uc2  The governor's projection compares with the bank's April forecast of 3.8 percent growth. But that was before the government announced first quarter GDP rose 4.6 percent on a year earlier. The economy contracted a provisional 5.8 percent in 1998 from 1997, its worst performance on record. \par \par \uc2  Rising imports on the back of the recovery would likely halve the 1999 current account surplus to around $20 billion, he said. \par \par \uc2  And while increases in public service and utility charges, coupled with rising oil prices, may cause consumer prices to edge up in the second half, consumer price inflation for the year should be held to the two percent level, Chon said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said South Korea would be wary about its macroeconomic policy to prepare for unexpected shocks, such as a slowdown in the global economy, a hike in U.S. interest rates or a devaluation of the Chinese yuan. \par \par \uc2  Chon said the government was trying to keep the surging won under control through indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as pre-paying foreign debt and calling in some of the $20 billion in loans the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended last year to domestic banks to ease a liquidity crunch in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The government is worried the robust won will hurt exports, which remain the economy's growth engine. \par \par \uc2  But Chon said the government first consults the International Monetary Fund if it is considering a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  The IMF required Seoul to adopt wide-ranging fiscal, financial and corporate reforms in exchange for a record $58.35 billion rescue package it arranged in December 1997. \par \par \uc2  "The government consults with the IMF before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market even for 'smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 '," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least three times last week, buying about $250 million to $260 million to prop up the dollar, market sources said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars shortly after the IMF's senior representative in Seoul, John Dodsworth, told Reuters it was not restraining \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth day-to-day volatility on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "There is an agreement between the government and the IMF concerning market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when it is direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Chon said. \par \par \uc2  But he added that the government's basic policy was to let market forces determine the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  South Korea would maintain its present monetary policy of stabilising short-term interest rates at their current levels during the second half of 1999, Chon said. \par \par \uc2  "Market interest rates should remain stable, by and large, in the second half of the year." \par \par \uc2  He said the bank would maintain money supply growth as measured by M3 at around 13 percent in the second half. \par \par \uc2  The governor also said he felt long-term interest rates were now at the right level in view of the recovering economy. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the projected GDP growth, and the expected inflation rate (of around three percent), the long-term equilibrium interest rate would come out very close to the current market rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  The three-year corporate bond yield closed at 8.04 percent on Monday, up two basis points for the day. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6e06lgm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
