\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea won ends barely lower on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1999\par 10:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, June 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  won closed barely down versus the dollar on Friday as a series of government measures including a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased earlier gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea declined to confirm its action just before the close of trade, but dealers said it was the third such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  The won finished at 1,167.6, compared with Thursday's close of 1,167.0. It opened at 1,169.0 and moved between 1,165.4 and 1,172.0. \par \par \uc2  "Interbank operators danced to the government's tune throughout the day, while corporations had a field day making profits by unwinding their dollar holdings," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in shortly before the closing and bought about $100 million. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came a little after the International Monetary Fund reaffirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced no restraints in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth day-to-day volatility on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Since the beginning of the IMF-supported programme, there has never been any intention to constrain the authorities in their day-to-day smoothing out of volatility of the exchange rate," the IMF's chief representative in Korea John Dodsworth told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on each of the past two days, buying more than $100 million on Thursday and $50 million to $60 million on Wednesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Korea is concerned the won's appreciation will hurt its export competitiveness and its nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger won makes it difficult for the country's exporters to compete in international markets, especially against Japanese goods. The two countries compete head to head on cars, ships and electronics. \par \par \uc2  South Korea's finance ministry on Friday acknowledged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been repeatedly buying dollars in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to contain the won's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The purpose is to maintain the supply and demand, to maintain a balanced market," ministry spokeswoman Kang Yeoun-sun told Reuters Television in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "Over a mid-term to long-term, we'd like to maintain a healthy amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and also maintain a positive current account balance for 1999," Kang said. \par \par \uc2  "The other thing is making sure there's fairness and prudential regulation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, a finance ministry official said the government was closely monitoring the movements of dollar/yen and dollar/won and the government intended to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately if the won showed signs of appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Traders said escalating tensions in the naval standoff between North and South Korea on the Yellow Sea also factored into the won's weakness. \par \par \uc2  South Korean navy vessels on Friday shoved four North Korean patrol ships out of the South's territorial waters, in an escalation of a five-day standoff in the Yellow Sea. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won's movements next week would depend heavily on the intensity of the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "But the government is unlikely to overturn the (dollar's bearish) trend," a dealer at a major domestic bank said. \par \par \uc2  The local dealer saw the won moving in a range of 1,165 to 1,175 for next week, while the European bank dealer gave a 1,155-1,170 range. \par \par \uc2  The six-month non-deliverable forward (NDF) won was quoted at 1,170/72 per dollar, while the one-year stood at 1,173/75. \par \par \uc2  ((Jean Yoon, Seoul Newsroom +822 727 5643, fax +822 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6b06lx2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
