\par\par \b \uc2  S. Korea Won Up Early; Extends Wed Gains On Yen's Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1999\par 03:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won is up early Thursday against the U.S. dollar, extending its sharp gains from Wednesday on the continued strength in the yen and amid the absence of significant dollar-buying by state-run banks, traders said. \par \par \uc2  After slipping momentarily to as low as 1,159 won (KRW) shortly after the opening of trading, at 0103 GMT (9:03 p.m. Wednesday EDT) the dollar was trading at KRW1,160, down from Wednesday's closing level of KRW1,165.50. That reflected a 13-won tumble from Tuesday's close and was the dollar's lowest finish against the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Jan. 20, when it closed at the same level. \par \par \uc2  The won/dollar exchange rate often tracks movements in the yen/dollar rate, as South Korea directly competes with Japan in roughly half of its exports. \par \par \uc2  The yen climbed against the dollar during Wednesday's New York trading session as expectations grew that Japan's first-quarter gross domestic product figures will reflect stronger growth than originally expected. Japan's GDP data are scheduled to be released Thursday at 0630 GMT. \par \par \uc2  At 0100 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y119.00, up slightly from Y118.98 late Wednesday in New York late Wednesday but down from Y119.40 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Despite comments Thursday by South Korean government officials that they are concerned about the won's sharp rise against the dollar, state-run banks weren't active dollar buyers early Thursday, traders said. Rumors circulated in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market late Wednesday that the Bank of Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said that if the government fails Thursday to support the dollar directly or indirectly, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could test the KRW1,150 level. \par \par \uc2  The volatility in the dollar's levels versus the won leaves traders leery of trying to project a precise trading range for Thursday. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain Thursday within a broad range of KRW1,150 to KRW1,170. \par \par \uc2  -By Louis Hau; 822-732-2165; lhau@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 10-06-99 \par \par \uc2  0125GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6a04est\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
