\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-S.Korea powerless to stop won climb. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jean Yoon \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1999\par 11:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, June 10 (Reuters) - Despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears powerless to stop the steady appreciation of the won because of International Monetary Fund restraints, analysts said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  After months of trying to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a brush with sovereign default in late 1997, South Korea is now worried it is climbing too fast against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second day in a row on Thursday to buy an estimated $100 million, ending the won's six-day rising streak. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the trend would not be reversed. \par \par \uc2  The won finished at 1,167.0 on Thursday, compared with Wednesday's close of 1,165.5. It has risen 1.7 percent since June 1 and 3.2 percent since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Korea, a major exporter, is concerned the rising won will hurt its competitiveness and stall its nascent economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger won makes it difficult for the country's exporters to compete in international markets, especially against Japanese goods. The two countries compete head to head on cars, ships and electronics. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the unit will strengthen further because players doubted the government's ability to reverse the won's bullish trend in the face of a heavy inflow of foreign direct and portfolio investments. \par \par \uc2  An official at the commerce ministry confirmed South Korea will continue to seek foreign direct investment despite concerns of a conflict with government efforts to curb rapid appreciation of the won. \par \par \uc2  The won was around 850 to the dollar before the country began sliding into Asia's financial crisis in July 1997. \par \par \uc2  "This does not mean the broad trend has been reversed," said a dealer at a major domestic bank. "It would be difficult for the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  like this everyday." \par \par \uc2  The $100 million purchase was almost double Wednesday's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dealers estimated at $50 million to $60 million. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard for the government to (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a big way) because they'd need to consult the International Monetary Fund," said a dealer at a French bank. \par \par \uc2  The IMF arranged a $58.35 billion rescue package in December 1997 for South Korea and most of the country's key economic policy decisions are made in consultation with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The IMF has said the government should only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. \par \par \uc2  "The government is likely to try to convince the IMF during its next policy meeting with Korea that it needs to be able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly," said Lee Chang-seon, an economist at LG Economic Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  Korea is set to meet with IMF representatives for two weeks starting from June 30 for a quarterly policy review. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the bank last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  between May 25 and May 31, buying $10 million to $20 million daily, but that the market had missed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the time because of the small transaction volume. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials refused to discuss the bank's trading activities. \par \par \uc2  The financial crisis was driven by a severe shortage of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that situation has been reversed. \par \par \uc2  South Korea posted a current account surplus of $8.80 billion in January-April against a $14.42 billion surplus a year earlier. Foreign direct investment plans in the first five months totalled $3.48 billion, up 93.3 percent from $1.80 billion in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also has been pressured recently by growing optimism over Japan's economic outlook. The dollar fell to 117.89/99 yen at 0715 GMT against 119.07/17 in late New York trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  An assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Tuesday Korea could easily halt the won's rise by buying dollars to build up reserves after Korea repays IMF loans in the second half. \par \par \uc2  Korea said in April it would buy $4.6 billion in the local market to keep the won from appreciating too much. \par \par \uc2  "How the government induces dollar demand is the key to slowing the pace of the dollar's fall," said Kim Hee-byung, a fixed income analyst at Samsung Securities. \par \par \uc2  "The government realises it can't reverse the trend but it will try to manipulate the market to manage the pace." \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6a06of0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
