\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -3:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Rumors Rife In Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 1999\par 11:02\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.6998, up from S$1.6984 in late Asian trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  While regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have seen marked appreciation, the Singapore dollar has seen less upward pressure because it is being used to fund regional carry trades. \par \par \uc2  "The interest rates are so low in Singapore that people are going short Singapore dollars to fund purchases in baht, rupiah or (Philippine) peso - \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher yields," a trader at a Japanese bank in Singapore said. "Without this element, the Singapore dollar would be much stronger." \par \par \uc2  Traders also said rumors abound that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has been actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market through local banks, in an effort to keep the U.S. dollar at or near S$1.70. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was also trading at 36.7350 Thai baht, unchanged from late Asian trading Tuesday. Despite the inactivity in the market, traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - as in Singapore and \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has become a fact of life in Thailand. \par \par \uc2  "Governments are concerned that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't strengthen too quickly, especially as funds pour into their domestic stock markets," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at a U.S. investment house. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand is thought to be using a number of foreign banks in Singapore as a quiet avenue for buying U.S. dollars. "It's a lucrative business for the foreign banks. They keep their mouths shut and get to keep tabs on what one of the largest players in the market is doing," said a trader at a U.S. bank. "Big advantage." \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the U.S. dollar was trading at 37.585 pesos, down from PHP37.610 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at NT$32.732, down from NT$32.735 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was at 1205.2 to the dollar, weaker than KRW1197.5 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  - By Brian Truscott; 65 421 4800; brian.truscott@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  05-12-99 \par \par \uc2  0502EDT \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv5c00vgw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
