\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to support dlr-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1999\par 03:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in Friday's morning trading session to keep the dollar from sliding further against the won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won was trading 1,162 per dollar at 0225 GMT after opening at 1,161 and moving between 1,150 and 1,170. \par \par \uc2  "The government's intentions are clear it would not allow the (dollar's) fall to continue and it has begun to take measures in earnest," said a foreign bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Government officals refused to comment on the central banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The government's official position on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always NCND (neither confirm nor deny)," a finance ministry official said. \par \par \uc2  The won has continued to gain against the dollar since late 1997 when it reached a low of 1,995 per dollar, mostly on corporate dollar sales and dollar inflows from foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  The government has raised concerns a strong won could hurt South Korea's exports, which compete mainly with Japan's exports, and dampen its efforts for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  ((Nick Yon, Seoul newsroom, +822 727 5644, fax +822 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1807256\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
