\par\par \b \uc2  SKorea exchange rate headed right way - BOK gov. \b0\par\par\uc2 211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 1998\par 06:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Nov 4 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but the won is moving in the right direction, Bank of Korea Governor Chon Chol-hwan said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Korea is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any way on the for. \par \par \uc2  "But the exchange rate is moving in the right direction," he added. \par \par \uc2  Responding to questions, Chon said he expected the South Korean economy to bottom out by the middle of next year "at the latest and head into recovery mode" as long as the external environment remained stable. \par \par \uc2  "The government does not expect a yuan devaluation," Chon said. \par \par \uc2  South Korea has not set any targets for the won/dollar rate or interest rates, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Since the outbreak of the crisis we have decided to let the markets determine everything," he said. \par \par \uc2  But the government was following some guidelines, including lowering interest rates as long as that did not jeopardise the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, Chon said. \par \par \uc2  South Korea also was keeping an eye on interest rate differentials between Korea and other countries and on capital flows, he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters newsroom +82-2-727-5640, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dub405gmm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
