\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Regionals firm as yen regains poise. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 1998\par 11:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 28 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended mostly firmer after a mixed day on Tuesday tracking the fortunes of the yen with Korean won still taking its own, more volatile road. \par \par \uc2  News from Tokyo that former Prime Minister Kiichi Miyazawa is likely to be the new finance minister gave the yen some strength after early weakness and the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed suit, though again activity is said to be light. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON SEES WILD SWINGS ON DAY \par \par \uc2  The won took a dramatic turn on Tuesday after rallying for several weeks against the dollar and pushing through 1,200 per dollar at one point to a high of 1185, it ended around 1,257 amid talk that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Strong bargain hunting and short-covering bids emerged after comments by an IMF official about a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the belief the dollar has hit its bottom," said a Korea Exchange Bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Talks of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were already circulating but got a critical boost when a senior International Monetary Fund official said outright an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was possible. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's Asia-Pacific department director Hubert Neiss told Reuters Television that there now was scope for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  He said with \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rebuilt to about $36 billion, "there is a possibility of careful, judicious (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least to smooth out day-to-day erratic movements". \par \par \uc2  Neiss also said South Korea had "turned the corner" in its balance of payments crisis, but that the appreciation of the won was raising concern. \par \par \uc2  He said there now was scope for the government to lower interest rates and for "judicious" \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth erratic day-to-day movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Korea has turned the corner as far as the balance of payments crisis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, is concerned and has now entered the restructuring phase in full," he said. \par \par \uc2  He added that with South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rebuilt to about $36 billion, "there is a possibility of careful, judicious (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least to smooth out day-to-day erratic movements". \par \par \uc2  This morning the Koreans released a new set of targets with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  These include a revision in the GDP forecast to a contraction of four percent. But the budget deficit target has been eased to four percent of GDP from 1.75 percent and new inflation and current account forecasts were set. \par \par \uc2  The economic target agreement means the Korean can expect another tranche of IMF money due in around mid-August. \par \par \uc2  IMF SEES 10,000 RUPIAH PER DLR STILL POSSIBLE \par \par \uc2  Neiss said on Tuesday an exchange rate of around 10,000 rupiah against the dollar was a "reasonable assumption" by the end of the current fiscal year next March. \par \par \uc2  He said the IMF and Indonesia had assumed in the government's economic programme that the rupiah would average 10,600 to the dollar during the fiscal year. He said this meant the rate would be around 10,000 towards the end of the fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  "I think this is still a reasonable assumption, or a possible assumption, and...prospects are now more favourable for a recovery of the rupiah because there will be ample inflows of foreign official money," he told Reuters Television in an interview. \par \par \uc2  RINGGIT SOFTER, KL SHARES DOWN ON ECONOMIC GLOOM \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was again looking softer and Kuala Lumpur shares hit a nine-year low as markets reacted to the shelving of plans to raise $2.0 billion dollars from the international bond market. \par \par \uc2  The lack of this funding has raised serious doubts about a scheme to shore up ailing banks, which are suffering under the weight of non-performing loans. \par \par \uc2  Today finance minister Anwar Ibrahim said the government was planning a series of more comprehensive measures to deal with the economic downturn later this week. \par \par \uc2  He didn't give any other details or an exact timing though, though speculation centres on another SRR cut. Bank Negara last cut bank reserve requirements to eight from 10 percent on July 1. \par \par \uc2  Worries about the economic outlook are not really moving the ringgit, which has traded in a range around 4.15 all week, but is having a big impact on the stock market. \par \par \uc2  The KLSE hit a fresh nine year low this morning, so below even the worst of last years Asian crisis. It broke through 400, bottomed out at around 399.23 and then recovered to be 402.30 by the mid-session break. \par \par \uc2  STATE OF THE UNION FAILS TO HELP MANILA MARKETS \par \par \uc2  In Manila worries about the economic outlook there have been taking a toll on the Peso today. This follows the first state of the nation address by the new president Joseph Estrada last night who painted a fairly poor economic picture and outlined few concrete proposals to deal with the problem. \par \par \uc2  Manila shares ended down nearly two percent today. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ..... \par \par \uc2   Change on the day... TUE 28 0941.GMT. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ....Latest.......Prev Close.....Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit.....4.12...........4.14...........0.55% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......13620.00.......13975.00.......2.61% \par \par \uc2   Baht........41.00..........41.10..........0.24% \par \par \uc2   Peso........42.07..........42.10..........0.07% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr....1.71...........1.72...........0.54% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr..34.38..........34.40..........0.07% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won..1249.00........1208.00.......-3.28% \par \par \uc2   ..... \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis..... \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ....Latest.......July '97.......Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit.....4.12...........2.52.........-38.80% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah......13620.00.......2432.00......-82.14% \par \par \uc2   Baht........40.95..........25.90........-36.75% \par \par \uc2   Peso........42.07..........26.50........-37.01% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr....1.71...........1.43.........-16.41% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr..34.38..........27.75........-19.27% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won..1249.00........842.00.......-32.59% \par \par \uc2   ************************************************* \par \par \uc2   STOCK MARKETS..... \par \par \uc2   Change on the day... TUE 28 0941.GMT. \par \par \uc2   Market......Current....Prev Close...Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Indonesia....483.53......482.98.......0.1% \par \par \uc2   Malaysia.....403.98......408.04......-1.0% \par \par \uc2   Philippines..1628.33.....1661.19.....-2.0% \par \par \uc2   Singapore....1060.14.....1067.81.....-0.7% \par \par 