\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Korea won up, overrides  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fear. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoo Choon-sik \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 07:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, July 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won hit a fresh year high against the dollar on Monday morning as steady dollar inflows overrode \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won ended the morning at 1,216 to the dollar after hitting a year high of 1,215. It opened at Friday's close of 1,245, which was a session low. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said talk of possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stop the won's rally, undesirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is struggling to boost exports. \par \par \uc2  "There's little possibility for the trend to turn around in the short run as the market is so heavy with dollars, while there is no demand," a Korea Exchange Bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Right now nothing but \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  can turn around the trend, but traders would not move until they actually see (dollar) bids coming in," said another dealer. \par \par \uc2  The won's strength has been underpinned by growing current account surpluses and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and active asset or stake sales by local companies. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the market has shrugged off recent comments by government officials about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and what dealers said were \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by state-run institutions in the market. \par \par \uc2  An official at the Bank of Korea said the bank had decided to absorb $273.16 million to be brought in by Commerzbank AG  of Germany to pay for a 32.39 percent stake in Korea Exchange Bank  early this week. \par \par \uc2  The official was confirming a report by the Maeil Business Newspaper that said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had decided to absorb the money to prevent the payment from flooding the market. \par \par \uc2  He said the bank was following regulations that require it to swap dollars brought in by local branches of foreign financial institutions into won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The German bank will become the second-largest shareholder in the Korean bank, with the government continuing to hold the biggest stake, of 33.62 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said various indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts by the government were noticed in the market but not independently confirmed. \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been reluctant to directly step into the market, dealers said state-run banks and corporations have been buying more dollars on the market than usual. \par \par \uc2  "It's a known secret on the market that government authorities have been buying dollars under the government's instruction," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  The rally of the won, which has gained more than 60 percent against the U.S. dollar since touching its record low of 1,995 late in December, is particularly unsettling for exporters. \par \par \uc2  A stronger won while the Japanese yen remains weak against the greenback makes South Korean exports expensive even without price changes in terms of won. \par \par \uc2  The two neighbouring countries compete head-to-head in major export markets for products ranging from electronic goods and computer chips to ships and automobiles. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts both at home and abroad see the won's rally to be temporary now that the country's trade balance is expected to worsen in coming months in line with shrinking exports. \par \par \uc2  But there is no consensus on how long the rally will last or where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hit bottom. \par \par \uc2  South Korea accepted a rescue package totalling a record $58.35 billion from the International Monetary Fund in December in exchange for painful economic reforms. ((Seoul Newsroom, +822 727 5646, fax +822 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7r007ka\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
