\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won firms on direct investment. \b0\par\par\uc2 836 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 1998\par 14:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 27 (Reuter) - The South Korean won surged to around a year high on Monday as direct investment by foreign corporates sucked dollars into the economy. \par \par \uc2  Most other emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed, with the rouble holding steady despite Russian debt prices hitting record lows. \par \par \uc2  The won was at 1,208 bid to the dollar at 1145 GMT, some five percent stronger than London's Friday close of 1,273. It earlier ended the day in Seoul at 1,209 after hitting a year high of 1,208. \par \par \uc2  "There's no demand really for dollars, no domestic demand for imports. But money is coming in from foreigners - corporates paying for companies or units of companies," said Paul McNamara, emerging markets economist at Julius Baer Investments in London. \par \par \uc2  Corporate restructuring in response to South Korea's financial crisis has resulted in a stream of equity stakes and company assets coming onto the market. \par \par \uc2  McNamara said South Korea's stiff market regulation meant dollar inflows from this direct investment were not being counterbalanced by portfolio outflows, hoisting the won to stronger levels than domestic demand would suggest. \par \par \uc2  An analyst at a U.S. bank in London, who asked not to be named, said South Korean fundamentals were also underpinning the won. \par \par \uc2  "Korea has just firmed incredibly," he said. "They're sticking to what the IMF wants, and taking a reasonably hard line against labour unrest." \par \par \uc2  Analysts were keeping an open mind on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the won's rise, which threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's competitiveness as it fights to boost exports, but said there was currently no word of such action. \par \par \uc2  "There's going to be official displeasure (at the stronger won) but there's not a great deal they can do about it," the U.S. analyst said. \par \par \uc2  He said one alternative to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - lowering interest rates - could be counterproductive by acting as a signal of economic confidence, which could in turn attract investment and lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The rouble, which weakened on Friday on the back of a cut in the benchmark refinancing rate to 60 percent from 80 percent, was stable at 6.272 bid to the dollar at 1145 GMT, virtually unchanged from Friday's London close of 6.26. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weathered the negative mood driving down Russian bonds, with benchmark PRINs\tab \tab   slipping 2-3/4 from Friday's close in New York to 41-1/8 bid, just above record lows of 40-1/2 earlier on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par McNamara said the free market in PRINs meant prices adjusted quickly to driving forces, notably a current glut of Russian paper. Trading in the rouble was less flexible in comparison, and the rescue package agreed with the International Monetary Fund was lending credibility to the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The rouble is defensible at these levels," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other analysts said the market was looking for a slight rouble weakening to around 6.30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ringgit also held steady, with analysts saying the market had foreseen the cuts in Malaysia's credit rating late last week by both Moody's Investors Service and Standard & Poor's.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The downgrade was already priced into the market," McNamara said. "There's nobody left to sell it."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The ringgit was at 4.14 to the dollar at 1145 GMT, against 4.143 late on Friday in London. Analysts saw some further weakening, with support around 4.20.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were little moved, staying within tight ranges.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS SNAPSHOT. The following is a snapshot of emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. * ASIA\tab   * Indonesian rupiah\tab   13,975 per dollar bid vs 14,000 late in London on Friday. * Malaysian ringgit   4.14 per dollar bid vs 4.143 * Thai baht\tab     at 41.1 per dollar vs 41.25 * Philippine peso\tab   unchanged at 42 per dollar * South Korean won\tab   at 1,208 per dollar vs 1,273 * Singapore dollar  1.724 per dollar versus 1.7105 * Indian rupee\tab   42.5 per dollar vs 42.45. * EUROPE\tab \tab  * Russian rouble\tab   at 6.272 per dollar bid vs 6.26 on Friday. * Zloty 8.44/8.41 percent above target basket parity vs 8.41/8.31 at the opening on Monday. * Mark/Czech crown \tab     at 17.669 bid vs 17.54 * Slovak crown\tab      19.433 on mark vs 19.36 * Hungarian forint\tab      121.22 vs mark against 120.98 * Ukrainian hryvnia\tab   at 2.1275 per dollar vs 2.1195 * Romanian leu\tab   at 8,700 per dollar vs 8,705 * AFRICA\tab \tab  & MIDEAST\tab    * Israeli shekel\tab   3.6543 bid on dollar from Friday's\par 3.6699 * South African rand\tab   at 6.265 per dollar vs 6.22 * Kenyan shilling\tab   unchanged at 59.05 * LATIN AMERICA\tab \tab * Mexican peso\tab   at 8.873 per dollar vs 8.863 * Brazil's real\tab   at 1.166 per dollar vs 1.1646 ((Gill Tudor, London newsroom +44 171 542 6762, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par