\par\par \b \uc2  Korea won ends slightly lower on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  woes. \b0\par\par\uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, July 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won closed slightly lower against the dollar on Tuesday as concerns about dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the lid on dollar sales stemming from heavy dollar positions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The won closed at 1,291 per dollar from Monday's close of 1,289. It opened at 1,292 and ranged from 1,287 to 1,293. \par \par \uc2  "Trading was dull because of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a domestic bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers forecast narrow-ranged trading on Wednesday in the absence of fresh factors. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Korea Exchange Bank said the won would move between 1,285 and 1,295 on Wednesday, while a Shinhan Bank dealer put the range between 1,280 and 1,300. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the won would strengthen further towards the end of the month due to rising exporter settlements. \par \par \uc2  "In addition, dollar inflows from asset sales by local companies to foreigners will likely continue," said a Citibank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a decision on Monday to relax curbs on dollar buying by corporations and individuals had little impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry said on Monday it would ease restrictions on dollar buying, effective from Wednesday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had significantly stabilised. \par \par \uc2  ((Kim Myong-hwan, Seoul Newsroom, +822 727 5645, fax +822 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7l00lfy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s