\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-S.Korea's won capped by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoo Choon-sik \par \uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1998\par 11:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, July 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's won retreated from its new yearly high on Thursday after the finance minister said the government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from becoming overvalued against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The won closed at 1,283 to the dollar against Wednesday's 1,282 finish, after touching an intra-year high of 1,265. It hit a low of 1,288. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the won, whose upsurge has spurred concerns over the country's export outlook, was unlikely to trend weaker against the dollar over the minister's remarks but would move in narrow ranges. \par \par \uc2  They said the won's recent strength was due to an unusually low demand for dollars, which is unlikely to change in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "The government is closely watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with concern and may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the market is in serious problems," Finance and Economy Minister Lee Kyu-sung told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The won has gained more than 50 percent versus the dollar since hitting a record low of 1,995 in late December. \par \par \uc2  The surge has been underpinned by growing current account surpluses and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which late last year, had plunged below a level equivalent to a month of imports. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea said the country's usable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $38.09 billion as of July 15, up from $37.04 billion at the end of June. \par \par \uc2  At the end of December, the country's usable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $8.87 billion after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poured dollars into the market in a failed attempt to defend its then plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dollars have streamed into the country with disbursements from an international aid package, a $4.0-billion overseas sovereign bond issue and a spate of major asset sales to foreigners by indebted Korean companies. \par \par \uc2  The extension of maturities on nearly $22 billion in short-term debt by international banks in March has also considerably eased the demand for dollars in the local market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Finance minister Lee said the government's stand would be to "neither confirm nor deny" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumours. His remarks reversed comments made on Monday when he said there would be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if the won rose above 1,300. \par \par \uc2  Lee said it was not appropriate for the government to "give signals on every movement in the exchange rate", indicating that a lack of comment would not exclude the possibility of an immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upsurge has raised concerns that the strong won could endanger the nation's efforts to overcome its worst economic crisis in decades through exports. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make the country's exports more expensive. \par \par \uc2  The won's strength is a big concern as the Japanese yen has weakened against the dollar this year. South Korea and Japan compete in major export markets for products ranging from electronic goods to automobiles and ships. \par \par \uc2  The financial crisis forced South Korea to accept a rescue package of $58.35 billion from the International Monetary Fund in December in exchange for painful economic reforms. ((Seoul Newsroom, +822 727 5646, fax +822 727 5666, seoul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7g00ss0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
