\par\par \b \uc2  S Korea Central Bk Govt/Won -2: No Comment On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 166 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 1998\par 09:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea Governor Chun Chul-hwan said Thursday the won is strengthening because of a temporarily excessive dollar supply, indicating the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to maintain its rising trend against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The won is up because of temporary excessive dollar supply, rather than a long-term expansion of overall dollar supply," Chun told local reporters, adding that he isn't in a position to say whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to devalue the won. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Kyu-sung said he's closely watching developments in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market where the won has been rising sharply against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The won closed Wednesday at a seven-month high of KRW1,282 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Chang Woo-hyuk; 822-732-2165; whchang@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-07-98 \par \par \uc2  0716GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du7g060s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
