\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: Indonesia Signs Fourth IMF Letter \b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1998\par 10:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Indonesia's government signed a fourth letter of intent with the IMF, paving the way for resumption of funding for its crippled economy. The agreement forecast a rupiah rate of IDR10,000 to the dollar by year-end, and predicted GDP will fall more than 10%, with 80% inflation, in the current fiscal year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep high interest rates and recapitalize, merge or close troubled banks. \par \par \uc2  The Asian Development Bank approved a $1.5 billion loan to reform Indonesia's financial sector. [*R/IO] \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar rose to Y141.65, its highest level since joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  June 17, before falling on worries of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At 0815 GMT, it was at Y140.79, down from Y141.23 late Wednesday in New York. U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Summers repeated that the U.S. will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  will auction bankrupt Kia Motors and Hanbo Steel, government officials said. The move is the latest in efforts to restructure the economy under the terms of a $58.35 billion IMF bailout. \par \par \uc2  The Financial Supervisory Commission said four or five troubled Korean commercial banks will merge with stronger ones through the purchase and assumptions method. [*R/SK] \par \par \uc2  -- The Bank of Japan will maintain its easy monetary policy, keeping its official discount rate at 0.5% and overnight call money rates below 0.5%. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan posted a merchandise trade surplus of Y222.60 billion in the first 10 days of June, up 53.6%. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's Prime Minister Hashimoto said he wants the proposed bridge bank to follow a U.S. formula, under which collapsed financial institutions temporarily would be under government control, Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. [*R/JA] \par \par \uc2  -- Several developers will repatriate more than HK$13 billion in offshore deposits after Hong Kong exempted interest income on local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits from profit taxes to ease a liquidity squeeze and lure back HK$200 billion located offshore, a local newspaper reported. [*R/HK] \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore is headed for a recession in 1998, and the economy will shrink more sharply than it did during the mid-1980s recession, a university economist said. [*R/SN] \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir said IMF-type policies damaged the economy, leading to 1.8% contraction. He also said APEC members must remind themselves APEC is about economic cooperation, not just opening markets. [*R/MY] \par \par \uc2  -- The government-supported Taiwan Institute of Economic Research will cut its GDP growth forecast to below 6%, according to a report by state-run Central News Agency. The cut will be the second this year. [*R/TW] \par \par \uc2  -- Asia's financial crisis has increased China's resolve to improve financial management and avoid risks in its banking sector, according to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official. [*R/CH] \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 25-06-98 \par \par \uc2  0843GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6p05fhj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
