\par\par \b \uc2  S.Korea mulls stronger  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market. \b0\par\par\uc2 138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SEOUL, Dec 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering further strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank won/dollar market to cap the won's sharp depreciation against the dollar, a finance ministry official said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea sold dollars in late afternoon trading to support the won, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not prevent the dollar from rising to its daily upper limit of 1,342.4 won. \par \par \uc2  "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was just for a warning," the official said. "We are watching the right timing to step into the market effectively." \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  complied with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) agreement which said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be limited to smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  -- SEOUL NEWSROOM (822) 727 5647 \par \par \uc2  -- email seoul.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc801gfv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
