\par\par \b \uc2  South Korea Unveils Measures Designed To Stem Financial Downturn \b0\par\par\uc2  By Cecilia M. Kang \par \uc2  Staff Reporter \par \uc2 1,083 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 1997\par 15:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL -(Dow Jones)- Determined to save its economy without turning to the International Monetary Fund, the South Korean government announced Wednesday a sweeping set of financial support measures aimed at stemming the country's deepening financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Although a step in the right direction, analysts and economists say the measures are too little too late, as IMF support appears the only way to restore foreign and domestic confidence in the local economy. \par \par \uc2  The government also softened its stance on requesting IMF support, but said it's confident it can bail itself out of the troubles before asking the organization for a helping hand. \par \par \uc2  Blamed for accelerating the country's financial woes, Kang Kyong-shik was dismissed from his position as Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Finance and Economy Wednesday. He was replaced by Minister of Trade Industry and Energy Lim Chang-ryul. \par \par \uc2  Lim immediately announced several much-anticipated measures aimed at reviving financial markets and the financial industry. Key measures include widening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band for the won to 10% from 2.25% above or below the day's average rate; boosting the government's bad debt fund to 10 trillion won from 3.5 trillion won; opening the country's $200 billion bond market to foreign investors; encouraging mergers and acquisitions among troubled merchant banks and banks, and allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to borrow $10 billion from foreign financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the measures are bold, but may be too late. "The measures are a step in the right direction, but they just aren't enough," says Chi Lo, regional economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Singapore. "The measures aren't strong enough to install foreign investors' confidence in the resolve of the government to restructure the financial industry." \par \par \uc2  Market experts also say thing's aren't expected to improve for the hard-hit South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the won expected to plunge Thursday to the bottom its widened trading band, or 10% below the market average rate. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plunged to its daily permissble low for the past three days, closing Wednesday at 1035.50 won to the dollar, compared with 1012.80 won Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Lo and others say the only way to regain foreign and domestic confidence in the local economy is for the South Korean government to buckle down and ask the IMF for support. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, newly chosen Finance Minister Lim indicated that South Korea could appeal for help to the U.S. and Japan. He urged both countries to cooperate with Korea's efforts to resolve its financial troubles, but didn't specify whether Korea had asked the two countries directly for support. He also didn't say whether the government plans to ask them for financial support. \par \par \uc2  Local newspaper reports, though denied by the Japanese and Korean governments, said South Korea is planning to ask the Bank of Japan for $20 billion to $30 billion in emergency funds. However, analysts said the Bank of Japan, facing its own financial strains, isn't in a position to help Korea. \par \par \uc2  Lim noted that as the world's 11th largest country, if South Korea's financial ills worsen, troubles will spread to the U.S. and Japan. "To the U.S., South Korea's its fifth-largest export country," Lim said, stressing the large influence Korea has on the U.S.'s economic stability. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, comments from the new finance ministry head indicated the government has eased its former hard-lined stance against turning to the IMF for support. "South Korea can solve (its financial crisis) if international financial institutions cooperate," said Lim. "But, if necessary, (South Korea) can consider request for IMF aid." \par \par \uc2  As for the reshuffling of key government officials, analysts say they doubt that alone will encourage investors. \par \par \uc2  "On the one hand, Kang Kyong-shik was hated by the business community so replacing him might boost some confidence," said a head of research at local brokerage house. "But on the other hand, Lim won't really change things from when things were run by Kang." \par \par \uc2  The research head added that the measures announced by Lim were basically devised by former finance minister Kang. He and other agree that Kang was used as a scapegoat for the country's economic ills, and removing him does little to restore confidence. \par \par \uc2  Kang is widely blamed for accelerating South Korea's financial woes with his actions in the Kia Group affair. He first announced the government wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the troubled group, \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's eighth largest congolmerate. But he eventually had to bail out the company with a state takeover by Korea Development Bank, as the crisis widened and affected other companies. \par \par \uc2  In addition to Kang's dismissal, Chief Presidential Economic Adviser Kim In-ho was fired and replaced by Kim Young-suk, chief of Korea's Custom's Administration. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the market has already factored in the financial support measures and resignation of Kang, therefore any lasting recovery in stock prices or fall in interest rates aren't expected. Shares ended 7.93 points higher at 502.59 on the benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index on anticipation of the measures, which were announced after the stock market's close. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates, measured by yields on three-year corporate bonds, rose 0.35 percentage point to a new 28-month high of 14%, on continued worries about liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  Although the government increased its fund meant to absorb non-performing debt held by local financial institutions, analysts still worry whether the fund is sufficient. \par \par \uc2  Lim said banks are suffering from bad loans totaling 28.53 trillion won as of the end of September, while merchant banks are buried under around four trillion won in nonperforming debt during the same period. \par \par \uc2  The country's total foreign debts, meanwhile, are expected to reach $110 billion by the end of the year, about half of which are short-term debts. \par \par \uc2  Lo said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing by the country's merchant banks reached $20 billion at the end of October. Of the total, $3.2 billion is short-term debts, which have been paid within a year. Analysts worry that as short-term debt matures, jittery foreign investors won't be willing to roll over their loans to Korean borrowers, which would trigger a massive credit crunch in the economy. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtbj0126o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
