\par\par \b \uc2  Review:Won's Fall Spotlights Korea Econ Weakness,Fuels Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 04:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Charles S. Lee \par \par \uc2  SEOUL (AP-Dow Jones)--South Koreans are calling it 'the four-digit era.' On Monday, the won closed at 999 to the U.S. dollar, pushing the rate that ordinary Koreans pay for dollars at banks to more than 1,000 for the first time ever. Local newspaper headlines heralded the new epoch curtly: '$1=W1,000.' \par \par \uc2  Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered a bit in subsequent days, the implications of its slide are stark. South Korea's economy is staggering through its most bruising slump in years, and there's a growing fear that it may become the next domino to fall to the financial turmoil shaking its Southeast Asian neighbours. If that happened, the consequences would reverberate not only through Asia but also around the globe, reports the latest edition of the Far Eastern Economic Review published Thursday. \par \par \uc2  South Korea is the world's 11th-biggest economy and its 12th-largest trading power. Its companies play a significant role in strategic industries from semi-conductors to cars to steel, and foreign bankers and investors are far more exposed to its troubles than they are to those of Thailand and Indonesia, both now under emergency care by the International Monetary Fund. A South Korean economic crash would certainly open a new, far more dangerous, chapter in the Asian confidence crisis, and one that could encompass its neighbours in Northeast Asia. \par \par \uc2  'Clearly, the market has taken the view that the Asian crisis isn't over,' says Steven Xu, a regional economist for Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. 'Korea is undermining everyone.' \par \par \uc2  The won recovered slightly by early Thursday on dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 South Korea\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the dollar was trading at 987.60 won, down from 988.80 won at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-11-97 \par \par \uc2  0357GMT \par \par \uc2  1135 4206 62556 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtbd08op6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
